{"id":"24e9ea75-a287-4955-a860-73d7b586e060","object":"chat.completion","created":1784808303,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    payload = case['payload']\\n    word_token_indices = payload['word_token_indices']\\n    word_labels = payload['word_labels']\\n    token_count = payload['token_count']\\n    if len(word_labels) != len(word_token_indices):\\n        return {\\\"valid\\\": False, \\\"error\\\": \\\"Mismatch between word_labels and word_token_indices length\\\"}\\n    token_to_label = [None] * token_count\\n    for i, indices in enumerate(word_token_indices):\\n        label = word_labels[i]\\n        for idx in indices:\\n            if not isinstance(idx, int) or idx < 0 or idx >= token_count:\\n                return {\\\"valid\\\": False, \\\"error\\\": f\\\"Token index {idx} out of range\\\"}\\n            if token_to_label[idx] is not None:\\n                return {\\\"valid\\\": False, \\\"error\\\": f\\\"Overlap at token index {idx}\\\"}\\n            token_to_label[idx] = label\\n    for idx, label in enumerate(token_to_label):\\n        if label is None:\\n            return {\\\"valid\\\": False, \\\"error\\\": f\\\"Token index {idx} uncovered\\\"}\\n    token_labels = [label for label in token_to_label]\\n    retained_indices = [i for i, label in enumerate(token_labels) if label == 1]\\n    return {\\\"token_labels\\\": token_labels, \\\"retained_indices\\\": retained_indices}\"}","reasoning_content":"We need to produce a JSON with key \"implementation\" containing Python source code for solve(case). The task: given word_token_indices (list of list of ints), word_labels (list of ints), token_count (int). Each word group has a list of token indices. Labels are per word group. We need to broadcast each word label to all token indices in that group. Then produce a full token-label vector of length token_count, where each token gets the label from the word group that includes it. Validate: no overlapping indices? The description says reject overlapping, out-of-range, or uncovered token indices. So we must check: each token index appears exactly once across all word groups? Or at least no overlap? \"Reject overlapping, out-of-range, or uncovered token indices.\" So we need to ensure that all token indices from 0 to token_count-1 are covered exactly once? Actually \"uncovered\" means some token indices are not in any group. So we must reject if any token index is missing. Also \"overlapping\" means a token index appears in more than one group. Also \"out-of-range\" means token index >= token_count or negative. So we must validate these conditions. Then if valid, compute the token-label vector (list of ints of length token_count) where each token gets the label of the unique word group it belongs to. Then we need to return the full token-label vector and retained indices (the indices where label equals 1). So output should be a dict with keys like \"token_labels\" and \"retained_indices\"? The description says: \"Return the full token-label vector and retained indices.\" So we need to return a JSON-serializable object. Probably a dict with two keys: token_labels (list of ints) and retained_indices (list of ints). Also maybe \"valid\": True? But they didn't specify to return a status. They said \"Reject overlapping, out-of-range, or uncovered token indices.\" That means if invalid, we should raise an exception? Or return something else? The solve function must return a JSON-serializable object. Typically in such tasks, if validation fails, we return an error object or raise? The instruction says \"must not mutate case\" and \"must return a JSON-serializable object\". It doesn't say to raise exceptions. Usually in these bounded tasks, they expect to handle invalid input gracefully, maybe return an object with an error key? But the description says \"Reject\" which could mean raise or return empty. However, since the function signature is solve(case) and must return JSON object, I'll assume we should validate and if invalid, return something like {\"error\": \"invalid input\"} or return empty lists? But the fixture shows valid cases only. I'll implement validation and if fails, raise ValueError? That would cause the test to fail. Better to return a dict with \"valid\": false and maybe \"error\" message. But the specification doesn't specify. I'll look at typical effectslice tasks: they usually expect to return the computed result, and if validation fails, they expect to raise an exception. However, the function must return a JSON-serializable object, not throw. I'll check the wording: \"Reject overlapping, out-of-range, or uncovered token indices.\" That suggests that the function should not proceed if invalid, and presumably the caller will handle it. In the context of NLP-LL2-01, it's about label alignment, so inputs are expected to be valid. I'll implement validation and if invalid, return an error dict. Or maybe we should just compute and if invalid, it's undefined? I think it's safer to validate and raise ValueError, but then the function wouldn't return JSON. The instruction says \"must return a JSON-serializable object\". So I'll return a result with an \"error\" key if invalid, otherwise the expected output.\n\nBut the bounded objective says \"Project word-level keep/drop labels onto registered subword spans and recover a faithful ordered token mask\". And \"Keep token indices whose broadcast label equals one.\" So we need to output token_labels and retained_indices. Also \"Require one word label for every registered word group.\" That means word_labels length must equal len(word_token_indices). That is a validation.\n\nAlso \"Complete alignment\" means all tokens are covered exactly once. \"Offset integrity\" means indices are in range. \"Stable order\" means indices are sorted? Not specified but likely the order of token indices in each group should be ascending, and groups themselves in order? They didn't specify, but we can assume that the word_token_indices might be in order of words, and token indices within each group are sorted. We don't need to reorder.\n\nWe'll implement a function solve(case) that takes case dict with keys payload: {word_token_indices, word_labels, token_count}. We'll extract them, validate, compute, return dict with token_labels and retained_indices.\n\nMake sure to use only standard library, no imports beyond maybe json? No need to import json because returning dict will be serialized by caller.\n\nImplementation details:\n- Get word_token_indices, word_labels, token_count from case['payload'].\n- Check lengths: len(word_labels) must equal len(word_token_indices). If not, return error.\n- Create an array token_to_label of length token_count, initially None.\n- For each word group i, for each token index idx in word_token_indices[i]:\n    - Check idx is integer, 0 <= idx < token_count. If not, error.\n    - If token_to_label[idx] is not None, that means overlap, error.\n    - Set token_to_label[idx] = word_labels[i].\n- After processing all groups, check if any token_to_label is still None (uncovered). If so, error.\n- Now token_to_label is the full token-label vector (list of ints).\n- Compute retained_indices = [i for i, label in enumerate(token_to_label) if label == 1].\n- Return {\"token_labels\": token_to_label, \"retained_indices\": retained_indices}.\n\nIf any validation fails, return {\"error\": \"description of error\"}? But the spec didn't specify. I'll return a dict with \"valid\": False and \"error\" string. However, typical effectslice expects a result regardless. I think it's safer to raise an exception. But the function signature says return JSON. I'll return an error dict. The test cases are valid, so it won't matter. I'll go with returning error dict on invalid.\n\nAlso note: \"Require one word label for every registered word group.\" Already checked.\n\nNow produce the JSON output with key \"implementation\" and value the Python source string escaped.\n\nThe Python source must be a valid function. I'll write it with proper indentation.\n\nI'll include a docstring maybe.\n\nMake sure to escape newlines as \\n, backslashes as \\\\, quotes as \\\".\n\nAlso ensure that the JSON string is valid. I'll produce the implementation string then put into JSON.\n\nLet me write the code:\n\n```\ndef solve(case):\n    payload = case['payload']\n    word_token_indices = payload['word_token_indices']\n    word_labels = payload['word_labels']\n    token_count = payload['token_count']\n    if len(word_labels) != len(word_token_indices):\n        return {\"valid\": False, \"error\": \"Mismatch between word_labels and word_token_indices length\"}\n    token_to_label = [None] * token_count\n    for i, indices in enumerate(word_token_indices):\n        label = word_labels[i]\n        for idx in indices:\n            if not isinstance(idx, int) or idx < 0 or idx >= token_count:\n                return {\"valid\": False, \"error\": f\"Token index {idx} out of range\"}\n            if token_to_label[idx] is not None:\n                return {\"valid\": False, \"error\": f\"Overlap at token index {idx}\"}\n            token_to_label[idx] = label\n    for idx, label in enumerate(token_to_label):\n        if label is None:\n            return {\"valid\": False, \"error\": f\"Token index {idx} uncovered\"}\n    token_labels = [label for label in token_to_label]\n    retained_indices = [i for i, label in enumerate(token_labels) if label == 1]\n    return {\"token_labels\": token_labels, \"retained_indices\": retained_indices}\n```\n\nBut the instruction says \"must not mutate case\". This function does not mutate case.\n\nAlso, they might expect returning only the two keys without \"valid\". Since test cases are valid, I can just return token_labels and retained_indices. I'll omit \"valid\" and error handling to simplify? But the requirement says \"Reject overlapping...\" which implies we need to handle invalid input. Since fixtures are valid, it's safe to assume we only get valid. However, to be robust, I'll include basic validation and return error. But the return type must be JSON-serializable. A dict with error is fine. The caller may not expect error key, but it's okay. I'll keep error handling but for valid cases it returns the expected dict.\n\nAlternatively, I could raise ValueError but then the function wouldn't return. That would break the JSON requirement. So error dict is better.\n\nNow produce the JSON.\n\nI'll write the entire response as a JSON object with key \"implementation\". The value is a string containing the Python source. I need to escape newlines and quotes.\n\nLet me construct the JSON manually.\n\nImplementation string (including newlines):\n\n```\ndef solve(case):\n    payload = case['payload']\n    word_token_indices = payload['word_token_indices']\n    word_labels = payload['word_labels']\n    token_count = payload['token_count']\n    if len(word_labels) != len(word_token_indices):\n        return {\"valid\": False, \"error\": \"Mismatch between word_labels and word_token_indices length\"}\n    token_to_label = [None] * token_count\n    for i, indices in enumerate(word_token_indices):\n        label = word_labels[i]\n        for idx in indices:\n            if not isinstance(idx, int) or idx < 0 or idx >= token_count:\n                return {\"valid\": False, \"error\": f\"Token index {idx} out of range\"}\n            if token_to_label[idx] is not None:\n                return {\"valid\": False, \"error\": f\"Overlap at token index {idx}\"}\n            token_to_label[idx] = label\n    for idx, label in enumerate(token_to_label):\n        if label is None:\n            return {\"valid\": False, \"error\": f\"Token index {idx} uncovered\"}\n    token_labels = [label for label in token_to_label]\n    retained_indices = [i for i, label in enumerate(token_labels) if label == 1]\n    return {\"token_labels\": token_labels, \"retained_indices\": retained_indices}\n```\n\nNow escape: replace backslash with double backslash? Actually in JSON string, backslash is escaped as \\\\, newline as \\n, quote as \\\". I'll do it carefully.\n\nI'll write it as a string literal in Python then produce JSON.\n\nBetter to construct the JSON programmatically in my mind.\n\nLet me produce the final JSON.\n\nThe outer JSON: {\"implementation\": \"...the escaped source...\"}\n\nI'll write the source with escaped characters.\n\nI'll ensure no trailing spaces.\n\nNow, I'll output exactly that."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":807,"completion_tokens":2724,"total_tokens":3531,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":768},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":2394},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":768,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":39},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}